From f5f31796aeaa1278dae36d75abe80e315f3b2db6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <68373859+tomaszstaniak@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 20:26:25 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] virgl.hidd: release what an ended program left on the host;
 wait longer for a slow host and recover

A program that ends without destroying its OpenGL context never calls the
driver again, so its context, textures and buffers stayed on the host until
the guest was restarted. Each winsys now remembers the program that last
used it (task and unique ID; for a Shell the command's segment list, which
the Shell holds only while the command runs). A small process looks once a
second for programs that have ended, and opening a new winsys looks too;
what such a program left is released. ENV:VirGL-Released counts it.

The wait for the host was about ten seconds, and a missed answer or Ctrl-C
marked the card broken until reboot. A drawing command may now take 120 s
and any other 30 s (ENV:VirGL-Timeout overrides, in ms). After 3 s
ENV:VirGL-Status says that the host is slow. A command that is given up on
stays published; the next caller takes its late answer and the card works
again. The program that gave up keeps running without a picture: Mesa ends
a program whose allocations fail, so it gets plain memory instead. What it
could not release while the card was busy is released later.

ENV:VirGL-TestHold is a test aid that delays one answer.
glstress takes a texture size.
